ld-school\" → \"现代风格\" (modern style) — inverted! old-school vs modern. FINDING DIRECTION. Correction: \"复古怀旧风\" or \"老派风格\".\n\nQ039: fine. \"Killed a boss while already dead.\" → \"在已死亡的状态下杀死一个BOSS。\" fine.\n\nQ040: \"Stabilized the Abashed Expanse to maintain it in orbit.\" → \"控制次元浮岛使其稳定在地面上。\" — \"in orbit\" (轨道上) vs \"在地面上\" (on the ground) — inverted location. FINDING DIRECTION. Correction: \"使其稳定在轨道上\".\n\nAlso \"Abashed Expanse\" → \"次元浮岛\" — proper noun translated differently; without external knowledge, source is a proper name; target names it \"dimensional floating island\" — that's a rendering, can't judge without context? \"Expanse\" = 广阔区域, not 浮岛. Hmm, in ToME the Abashed Expanse is a zone. Translation \"次元浮岛\" — questionable but rule 1 says only objective issues from visible text. \"Expanse\" ≠ 浮岛 (floating island). But proper nouns are often localized creatively. Could be UNCERTAIN. The orbit/ground is clear FINDING. Maybe also UNCERTAIN for the name. I'll include one candidate for orbit/ground, maybe second UNCERTAIN for name. Actually adding it might be over-reporting. The main defect is orbit→ground. I'll include just that.\n\nQ021 correction: \"你看不懂的一种语言\" — target structure: \"which you do not understand\" modifies language. Correction: \"即使你不懂这种语言，也无法理解：...\" Hmm actually source fragment \"which you do not understand: 'Sho ch'zun...'\" — the text is in a language you don't understand. Target says even if you understand it you can't understand. Correction: \"这种语言你无法理解\" or \"你看不懂这种语言：\". I'll write: \"你无法理解这种语言：\"\n\nQ023: \"peer out\" — eyes look out through slot. \"凝视着他\" asserts gaze at \"him\". Correction: \"一双狂乱的眼睛向外窥视\". Verdict FINDING, claim ACTION or REFERENT. I'll use REFERENT? The added \"他\" plus direction. Use ACTION: peer out = 向外窥视, not staring at him. Actually two aspects: added object \"他\" and direction outward. I'll use DIRECTION or REFERENT. Pick REFERENT with correction removing 他.\n\nHmm, is it strong enough? \"peer out\"